---
import Chevron from "../../icons/Chevron.astro";

/**
 * "Standards We Refuse to Drop" — a genuinely NEW homepage section, not
 * a conversion of an existing one, despite how the original brief for
 * this described it ("convert the current static 8-item, 2-row grid
 * into a carousel"). Checked before writing any code, not assumed: a
 * full grep across every branch (local and remote), the entire commit
 * history, and every existing homepage component found no trace of this
 * section anywhere in the repo — flagged to the user directly, who
 * confirmed "you're right, build it fresh, not a conversion" and
 * supplied the real eyebrow/heading/background and all 8 card pairs
 * used below. Built directly in its final CAROUSEL form — there was
 * never an intermediate static grid to build first and then convert.
 *
 * SECTION BACKGROUND — solid `bg-ink` (`#0D0D0D`), `on-dark` for the
 * focus ring. This is a GENUINELY NEW use of Ink, not a colour already
 * verified for this role: every prior `bg-ink`/Ink-adjacent usage in
 * this codebase is either Hero's own translucent `bg-ink/28` tint
 * overlay, a small UI mark (NavDrawer's hamburger bars, Divider's `ink`
 * tone), or `Button.astro`'s `secondary`-variant FILL (a small element,
 * not a whole section) — never a full, opaque section-level background
 * before this. Contrast re-derived fresh for this specific role, not
 * assumed to carry over from any of those: white/Paper text on Ink is
 * ~19.44:1 (the exact same pairing already documented in global.css's
 * own `:focus-visible` comment, "Ink on Paper is ~19.44:1," just the
 * two colours swapped — contrast is symmetric between any two colours
 * regardless of which is foreground) — trivially AAA for both the
 * heading and the `text-paper/70` secondary text (computed
 * independently below, not assumed to inherit the plain-white number).
 * `on-dark` is required here, checked not assumed: the sitewide default
 * focus ring is Ink itself (global.css's own `:focus-visible` rule) —
 * an Ink ring on an Ink background is a literal 1:1 contrast ratio,
 * completely invisible, the most extreme version of the exact problem
 * `.on-dark` exists to solve. Paper ring on Ink: the same ~19.44:1 as
 * the text above, comfortably safe.
 *
 * `text-paper/70` (SECONDARY TEXT, the eyebrow and every card's body
 * copy) — computed fresh for THIS background, not assumed from
 * Footer.astro's own use of the identical class on a DIFFERENT dark
 * fill (`--color-footer-grey`, a lighter neutral than Ink): white at
 * 70% opacity composited over Ink's own sRGB (13,13,13) → composite
 * ≈0.715 → linearised ≈0.470 → contrast against Ink's own linear
 * luminance (≈0.004) ≈ (0.470+0.05)/(0.004+0.05) ≈ 9.6:1 — clears the
 * full 4.5:1 normal-text floor with wide margin, not a thin pass.
 *
 * EYEBROW — "So... why choose us?", plain sentence case with real
 * punctuation, NOT tracked-caps/ALL-CAPS. CLAUDE.md's own hard rule
 * bans tracked-caps eyebrow labels above headings by default, with
 * MissionBand's "Our mission" as the one existing named exception — but
 * this copy was never given in that ALL-CAPS/tracked shape to begin
 * with, so this doesn't need to invoke that rule's own override clause
 * at all; it's rendered as a plain, unstyled-beyond-size-and-colour
 * caption `<p>`, matching `SectorsGrid.astro`'s own "Retail security"-
 * style captions rather than MissionBand's tracked exception. Rendered
 * directly (not via `SectionHeading`, which has no eyebrow slot at all
 * by design — see that component's own comment) as a plain sibling `<p>`
 * above a plain `<h2>`, the exact same structure `MissionBand.astro`
 * already uses for its own caption+headline pair, for the same reason:
 * `SectionHeading` can't express this shape.
 *
 * HEADING ALIGNMENT — left, the sitewide default (`CLAUDE.md`'s "Left-
 * aligned by default" hard rule) — the brief didn't ask for a centred
 * treatment here, unlike `SectorsGrid.astro`'s own narrow, explicitly-
 * instructed exception, so this stays on the default rather than
 * inventing a THIRD centring case with no instruction behind it.
 *
 * CARDS — 8, headline + one-sentence body, no icon (none was supplied
 * or asked for; `SectorsGrid.astro`'s own "flag if icons are actually
 * wanted" precedent applies the same way here — flagging rather than
 * adding six-plus new bespoke icon files as unrequested scope). Base
 * (inactive) treatment: a thin `border-paper/15` hairline + a very
 * faint `bg-paper/3` wash, just enough to read as a distinct card
 * against the Ink section fill without competing with the active
 * card's own highlight. Deliberately NOT `Divider.astro`'s own `paper`
 * tone (`border-paper/35`) — checked, not assumed to be the same
 * value: that opacity was calibrated for an actual visible SEPARATOR
 * rule doing real dividing work, a different visual job from this
 * card's own quiet edge, which never needs to carry a WCAG floor at
 * all (a card's identity is its heading/body text, not its border —
 * decorative, exempt from 1.4.11 the way `Divider`'s own real rules
 * aren't). `border-paper/15` computed anyway for the record: ≈1.50:1
 * against Ink, well under the 3:1 non-text floor, confirming it reads
 * as the faint edge it's meant to be, not a component genuinely
 * relying on being seen.
 *
 * ACTIVE/CENTRED-CARD HIGHLIGHT — thin `border-amber` (2px, matching
 * `SectorsGrid.astro`'s own `border-b-2` active-tab weight, not a
 * heavier "call it out" border) + `shadow-lg shadow-amber/15` + a
 * slightly brighter `bg-paper/5` wash (up from the base `bg-paper/3`) —
 * deliberately NOT a solid Electric-Blue/Magenta fill (both are
 * reserved for `ServicesGrid.astro`/`SectorsGrid.astro`'s own panel
 * fills, per CLAUDE.md's own scope note) and NOT `Button.astro`'s own
 * Amber FILL (reserved sitewide for the one actual button/CTA
 * component). Contrast for the border itself (a bare, non-text mark,
 * the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 floor applies, not 4.5:1): Amber vs Ink ≈9.05:1 —
 * the SAME pairing already documented in global.css's own Amber token
 * comment ("Ink on this fill: ~9.05:1"), reused directly since contrast
 * between two colours doesn't depend on which one is the border and
 * which is the background. Comfortably clears 3:1 with the largest
 * margin of any bare-mark Amber use on this site (the header nav/
 * MissionBand underline's own accepted ~2.15:1-on-Paper case is the
 * one this project has actually had to accept a THIN margin on — this
 * one needs no such tradeoff). This is a genuine THIRD instance of
 * Amber's already-established "bare, non-text mark" use (nav active-
 * link underline, MissionBand's underline rule), not a new kind of
 * exception requiring fresh review — see the colour table's own Amber
 * row and global.css's own token comment, both updated in this same
 * change to name it. The box-shadow itself is pure decoration on top
 * of that already-sufficient border cue, not a second thing carrying
 * its own contrast requirement (WCAG doesn't require a shadow to be
 * perceivable — it's a depth cue, not the state indicator).
 *
 * DOES THIS READ AS A BUTTON/CTA? Checked deliberately before shipping,
 * per the brief's own explicit ask. Every existing Amber use on this
 * site is either a SOLID fill (every real button/CTA, always Ink text
 * on top) or a bare UNDERLINE beneath clickable nav text — this is
 * neither: a thin BORDER/RING around an entire non-interactive content
 * card, with no fill change and no text-colour change of its own. The
 * closest real precedent for "a thin Amber border marking a state, not
 * a click target" is `SectorsGrid.astro`'s own active-tab
 * `border-amber` underline — already an established, understood
 * "this one is currently selected" signal on this site, not "click
 * this" (the tab bar's OWN click targets are the buttons themselves,
 * unstyled by their border colour). This card's border reads the same
 * way — a state indicator, not an affordance — and, unlike the tab
 * bar, these cards aren't even clickable at all (no `<a>`/`<button>`
 * wrapper, no cursor change), which removes any residual "is this
 * interactive" ambiguity the tab-bar case didn't have to answer. No
 * change made purely on this reasoning — flagged and confirmed as
 * intended, matching the brief's own framing.
 *
 * "NO ARROWS APPENDED TO LINK/BUTTON TEXT" — CLAUDE.md's hard rule bans
 * a decorative arrow glyph tacked onto a text link/button's own copy
 * ("All services →"). The Prev/Next controls below are a different
 * thing entirely: standalone, icon-only, `aria-label`led NAVIGATION
 * controls with no text of their own for an arrow to be "appended" to —
 * the rule's own target (and `Button.astro`'s own "never add an arrow"
 * note) is specifically about ornamenting a link/button's TEXT, not
 * banning directional icons as functional pagination controls in
 * general. Flagged here so this doesn't get mistaken for a violation on
 * a future pass.
 *
 * LAYOUT/RESPONSIVE — plain flex row (`[data-standards-track]`),
 * `overflow-x-auto`, `snap-x snap-mandatory`, each card `snap-center`
 * and `shrink-0`. Card width, not item COUNT, drives how many are
 * visible: `w-full` (mobile, exactly one card, no partial peek — the
 * below-row Prev/Next buttons already signal "there's more" explicitly,
 * unlike `SectorsGrid.astro`'s own tab row, which had no other
 * affordance and needed an edge-fade `mask-image` for that reason
 * alone) → `sm:w-[calc((100%-1.5rem)/2)]` (two visible, one `gap-6`
 * between them) → `lg:w-[calc((100%-3rem)/3)]` (three visible, two
 * `gap-6`s between them, per the brief's own explicit "3 cards visible
 * on desktop"). Every width is an exact `calc()` against the track's
 * own real `gap-6` (1.5rem — global.css's own comment confirms the
 * plain numeric Tailwind spacing scale below section level, which this
 * `gap-6` is drawn from, is unmodified from Tailwind's own default), so
 * N cards plus (N-1) gaps always
 * fill the row exactly, with the (N+1)th card's own edge starting
 * flush at the row's right edge, ready to scroll into view — not an
 * approximate percentage that would leave a sliver of a 4th card
 * showing at `lg:`.
 *
 * ARROWS — one real Prev/Next button PAIR, not four (a naive "one
 * mobile-only pair, one desktop-only pair" duplicate would double the
 * DOM nodes needing `disabled`-state sync and double the `aria-label`s
 * to keep in agreement). Instead: a single wrapper `<div>` that's a
 * plain centred flex row BELOW the track on mobile (`flex md:contents`)
 * — matching the brief's own "arrows appear BELOW the card row" mobile
 * spec — and, at `md:`+, becomes `display:contents` (its own box drops
 * out of layout entirely, promoting its two button children to be
 * direct flow participants of the OUTER `relative` wrapper one level
 * up). Each button then gets `md:absolute` with `md:left-0`/`md:right-
 * 0` plus its own half-width negative/positive inline translate (`md:
 * -translate-x-1/2` on the left button, `md:translate-x-1/2` on the
 * right one) to sit half outside the row's own edge — so roughly half
 * of each 44px button sits inside the track's
 * own padded edge and half sits out in the section's own `px-gutter`/
 * `md:px-12` gutter — comfortably inside the 48px `md:px-12` gutter
 * width, confirmed against global.css's own `--spacing-gutter`
 * definition before choosing this offset) and `md:top-1/2 md:
 * -translate-y-1/2`, vertically centred against whatever height the
 * ONE relative ancestor (the track alone, not the whole section) ends
 * up — satisfying "vertically centred against the row" exactly, not
 * against the section as a whole.
 *
 * DISABLED-END STATE — no infinite loop; disabled/greyed at either end,
 * per the brief's own default, with the reasoning for choosing that
 * over looping recorded in `standardsCarousel.ts`'s own top-of-file
 * comment (a finite "read through 8 items" list, not a rotating
 * showcase — not repeated here).
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY — `role="group"` + `aria-label` on the track (a
 * deliberately lighter pattern than the full WAI-ARIA APG "Carousel"
 * role, which assumes auto-advancing slides with live-region
 * announcements; this carousel is entirely user-driven, so that
 * pattern's own machinery would be more than this needs — flagged as a
 * judgment call, not silently picked), `tabindex="0"` so it's a real
 * keyboard-focusable region, and a `keydown` listener for ArrowLeft/
 * ArrowRight (see `standardsCarousel.ts`'s own comment for why this
 * overrides the browser's own smaller default arrow-key scroll
 * increment rather than relying on it). Both buttons carry real
 * `aria-label`s ("Previous standard"/"Next standard") since neither has
 * visible text of its own, and `disabled` (not just a visual dim) at
 * either end removes that button from the tab order there too.
 *
 * MOTION — no new bespoke module. `[data-reveal]` on the eyebrow+
 * heading block and again on the track — the SAME sitewide generic
 * fade/rise `scrollReveal.ts` already provides, already wired
 * unconditionally into `index.astro`'s own `initScrollReveal()` call
 * (no-argument, queries the whole document) — so this needed ZERO new
 * script wiring, just the attribute. The brief's own "preserve existing
 * entrance motion... adjust it only if genuinely required" instruction
 * assumed a PRE-EXISTING motion module to preserve; since this section
 * never existed before this change, there was nothing to preserve —
 * flagged rather than silently building a bespoke module to satisfy
 * language that didn't actually apply. A bespoke module the way
 * `SectorsGrid.astro`/`MissionBand.astro`/`ServicesGrid.astro` each
 * needed one wasn't warranted either: none of those three needed
 * something DIFFERENT because of an arbitrary rule that every section
 * gets its own unique motion — each needed it because its own layout
 * genuinely couldn't be served by a plain fade/rise (a tab bar, a
 * clip-path image unveil, six independently-triggered cards). A plain
 * heading + a plain horizontally-scrollable list of cards is exactly
 * the shape `scrollReveal.ts` was already built for (the same shape
 * `StatStrip.astro`'s own three columns use it for).
 */

interface Standard {
  headline: string;
  body: string;
}

const STANDARDS: Standard[] = [
  {
    headline: "Licensed Guards",
    body: "Every officer holds a valid SIA licence and the correct training for their role, including first aid.",
  },
  {
    headline: "Fair Pricing",
    body: "Cost efficiency matters to us, so we price competitively without compromising the standard of cover.",
  },
  {
    headline: "Bespoke Cover",
    body: "We build the shift pattern and team around your requirement rather than forcing you into a package.",
  },
  {
    headline: "Professional Standards",
    body: "Smart uniform, mature conduct and clear reporting on every deployment — no exceptions.",
  },
  {
    headline: "Support 24/7",
    body: "Our lines and our officers are available every hour of the week, all year round.",
  },
  {
    headline: "Punctual Staff",
    body: "Our people arrive early, briefed and ready for the shift they have committed to.",
  },
  {
    headline: "Real Experience",
    body: "Our crew have worked stadiums, marathons, events, retail floors, pubs and club doors.",
  },
  {
    headline: "A Diverse Team",
    body: "We hire on skill and character, welcoming people from every background into the crew.",
  },
];
---

<section class="on-dark bg-ink">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-gutter py-24 md:px-12">
    <div data-reveal>
      <p class="text-caption text-paper/70">So... why choose us?</p>
      <h2 class="text-h2 text-paper mt-4">Standards We Refuse to Drop</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="relative mt-12">
      <div
        data-standards-track
        role="group"
        aria-label="Standards we refuse to drop"
        tabindex="0"
        data-reveal
        class="standards-track flex snap-x snap-mandatory gap-6 overflow-x-auto scroll-smooth"
      >
        {
          STANDARDS.map((standard) => (
            <div
              data-standards-card
              class="standards-card w-full shrink-0 snap-center border border-paper/15 bg-paper/3 p-8 transition-[border-color,box-shadow,background-color] duration-300 ease-out sm:w-[calc((100%-1.5rem)/2)] lg:w-[calc((100%-3rem)/3)]"
            >
              <h3 class="text-h3 text-paper">{standard.headline}</h3>
              <p class="text-caption text-paper/70 mt-3">{standard.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 flex items-center justify-center gap-4 md:contents">
        <button
          type="button"
          data-standards-prev
          aria-label="Previous standard"
          class="grid size-11 shrink-0 place-items-center border border-paper/25 text-paper transition-colors duration-200 ease-out hover:border-paper/50 disabled:opacity-30 disabled:pointer-events-none md:absolute md:top-1/2 md:left-0 md:-translate-x-1/2 md:-translate-y-1/2"
        >
          <Chevron class="size-5 rotate-180" />
        </button>
        <button
          type="button"
          data-standards-next
          aria-label="Next standard"
          class="grid size-11 shrink-0 place-items-center border border-paper/25 text-paper transition-colors duration-200 ease-out hover:border-paper/50 disabled:opacity-30 disabled:pointer-events-none md:absolute md:top-1/2 md:right-0 md:translate-x-1/2 md:-translate-y-1/2"
        >
          <Chevron class="size-5" />
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Second consumer of this hidden-scrollbar technique —
     `SectorsGrid.astro`'s own comment on its identical style block
     explicitly names "kept local rather than promoted to a shared
     global.css utility until a second consumer actually needs it."
     That threshold is now met; NOT promoted in this change regardless,
     since doing so means editing SectorsGrid.astro's own working code
     as a side effect of an unrelated task — flagged here as a real
     promotion candidate for a future pass, not silently duplicated
     without comment. */
  .standards-track {
    scrollbar-width: none;
    -ms-overflow-style: none;
  }

  .standards-track::-webkit-scrollbar {
    display: none;
  }
</style>
